e balance of those components affects its properties and usefulness for plant growth.","heading":"Core idea"},{"content":"Compare soil samples and relate observable properties to composition and water behaviour.","heading":"Study focus"},{"content":"How might two soil types differ in water retention?","heading":"Check your understanding"}],"source_pages":[12],"source_url":"https://ncdc.go.ug/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/NCDC_AEP_BIOLOGY_LSC_level-1and-2_-Final-2024-Web-file_Final-08.03.2024.pdf"},{"source_document_id":"ncdc-aep-biology-lsc-level-1-2-2023","level_label":"Level 1","title":"Soil Erosion and Conservation: Causes, Effects and Prevention","summary":"Soil erosion can remove fertile soil; conservation practices aim to protect the land and maintain soil fertility.","learning_outcomes":["Identify possible causes and effects of erosion and compare practical ways to reduce soil loss."],"study_sections":[{"content":"Soil erosion can remove fertile soil; conservation practices aim to protect the land and maintain soil fertility.","heading":"Core idea"},{"content":"Identify possible causes and effects of erosion and compare practical ways to reduce soil loss.","heading":"Study focus"},{"content":"Suggest one way to reduce soil erosion in a local area.","heading":"Check your understanding"}],"source_pages":[14],"source_url":"https://ncdc.go.ug/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/NCDC_AEP_BIOLOGY_LSC_level-1and-2_-Final-2024-Web-file_Final-08.03.2024.pdf"},{"source_document_id":"ncdc-aep-biology-lsc-level-1-2-2023","level_label":"Level 1","title":"Transport in Animals","summary":"Animals have specialised transport systems that distribute useful materials to cells and carry away wastes.","learning_outcomes":["Describe the role of the main parts of an animal transport system and the materials it moves."],"study_sections":[{"content":"Animals have specialised transport systems that distribute useful materials to cells and carry away wastes.","heading":"Core idea"},{"content":"Describe the role of the main parts of an animal transport system and the materials it moves.","heading":"Study focus"},{"content":"Name two kinds of material transported around an animal''s body.","heading":"Check your understanding"}],"source_pages":[25],"source_url":"https://ncdc.go.ug/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/NCDC_AEP_BIOLOGY_LSC_level-1and-2_-Final-2024-Web-file_Final-08.03.2024.pdf"},{"source_document_id":"ncdc-aep-biology-lsc-level-1-2-2023","level_label":"Level 1","title":"Transport in Plants","summary":"Plants move water, mineral substances and manufactured food through specialised tissues and structures.","learning_outcomes":["Identify the materials transported in plants and explain the roles of transport tissues."],"study_sections":[{"content":"Plants move water, mineral substances and manufactured food through specialised tissues and structures.","heading":"Core idea"},{"content":"Identify the materials transported in plants and explain the roles of transport tissues.","heading":"Study focus"},{"content":"Why do plants need internal transport systems?","heading":"Check your understanding"}],"source_pages":[23],"source_url":"https://ncdc.go.ug/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/NCDC_AEP_BIOLOGY_LSC_level-1and-2_-Final-2024-Web-file_Final-08.03.2024.pdf"},{"source_document_id":"ncdc-aep-biology-lsc-level-1-2-2023","level_label":"Level 2","title":"Aerobic and Anaerobic Respiration","summary":"Respiration releases usable energy from food; aerobic and anaerobic processes differ in whether oxygen is used and in their products.","learning_outcomes":["Compare the two processes and explain why cells need energy."],"study_sections":[{"content":"Respiration releases usable energy from food; aerobic and anaerobic processes differ in whether oxygen is used and in their products.","heading":"Core idea"},{"content":"Compare the two processes and explain why cells need energy.","heading":"Study focus"},{"content":"What is one key difference between aerobic and anaerobic respiration?","heading":"Check your understanding"}],"source_pages":[29],"source_url":"https://ncdc.go.ug/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/NCDC_AEP_BIOLOGY_LSC_level-1and-2_-Final-2024-Web-file_Final-08.03.2024.pdf"},{"source_document_id":"ncdc-aep-biology-lsc-level-1-2-2023","level_label":"Level 2","title":"Asexual Reproduction in Plants (Vegetative Reproduction)","summary":"Some plants can form new independent plants from vegetative parts without fusion of reproductive cells.","learning_outcomes":["Identify plant parts used in vegetative propagation and compare examples of this method."],"study_sections":[{"content":"Some plants can form new independent plants from vegetative parts without fusion of reproductive cells.","heading":"Core idea"},{"content":"Identify plant parts used in vegetative propagation and compare examples of this method.","heading":"Study focus"},{"content":"Name a plant part that can produce a new plant vegetatively.","heading":"Check your understanding"}],"source_pages":[43],"source_url":"https://ncdc.go.ug/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/NCDC_AEP_BIOLOGY_LSC_level-1and-2_-Final-2024-Web-file_Final-08.03.2024.pdf"},{"source_document_id":"ncdc-aep-biology-lsc-level-1-2-2023","level_label":"Level 2","title":"Associations in Biological Communities","summary":"Organisms interact in habitats in different ways, and these relationships can affect access to resources and survival.","learning_outcomes":["Describe examples of interactions and compare their effects on the organisms involved."],"study_sections":[{"content":"Organisms interact in habitats in different ways, and these relationships can affect access to resources and survival.","heading":"Core idea"},{"content":"Describe examples of interactions and compare their effects on the organisms involved.","heading":"Study focus"},{"content":"Give one example of an interaction between organisms in a habitat.","heading":"Check your understanding"}],"source_pages":[54],"source_url":"https://ncdc.go.ug/wp-content/uploa